{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>You shall not curse Elohim</b> Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 22:27) translated it as referring to the judge — that one must not curse him when he holds him liable in judgment. לא תקיל (la taqil) is an expression of cursing in Aramaic, as in מקל להו (meqel lehu), \"May YHWH cut off the man who does this, answering and witnessing\" (Malachi 2:12), and so there are many instances in the language of the Jerusalem Targum." "<b>And a nasi among your people you shall not curse</b> — the one exalted over the people, and that is the king. And Scripture warned that one must not curse him when he holds him liable in his judgment. Now according to the opinion of our Sages in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 66a), <b>You shall not curse Elohim</b> is a warning against blessing (i.e., pronouncing the Name) of HaShem, even by a euphemism. And so Scripture warned concerning the King on high, blessed be He, and concerning the one who reigns on earth. And they said in the Gemara (there) that included within the term אלהים (Elohim) are the honored Name and the judge who sits in the seat of Elohim on earth. And it was not explained whether the Nasi, the head of the Great Sanhedrin, who is called Nasi in the Gemara (there 19a and elsewhere), enters into the category of the prohibition. And the Rav, Rabbi Moshe, said that he is included in this warning (Maimonides, Book of Commandments, negative 316, and in Laws of Sanhedrin 26:1). And so it appears to me from what Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi asked concerning himself, such as \"What am I — a Sa'ir...\" and so forth (Horayot 11b). And if so, the verse states: you shall not curse any nasi among the people who is a head of authority over all Israel, whether that authority be in the governance of the kingdom or whether it be the governance of Torah, for the Nasi of the Sanhedrin holds the supreme rank in the authority of Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The phrase מקל להו is the Aramaic of Malachi 2:12 as rendered in Targum Jonathan; the translation renders it literally as Ramban cites it.", 1 "The phrase ברכת השם literally means 'blessing of the Name'; it is the standard rabbinic euphemism for pronouncing a curse using the divine Name. The word Sa'ir (שעיר) refers to the goat offered on the inner altar, cited from Horayot 11b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:34.489864263Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}